{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='site:forum.intervals.icu \\\"Instant HR\\\" \\\"October\\\" 2026' numResults=8\"","aggregated_output":"Title: Upload planned workouts to Garmin Connect - Page 6 - Announcements - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/upload-planned-workouts-to-garmin-connect/1521?page=6\nPublished: 2021-10-07T06:13:03.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n# Upload planned workouts to Garmin Connect \n\n BUSHA 7 October 2021 06:13 101 \n\nThank you for the fast response. Yeah, it works!\n\n Gabor_Ziegler 8 December 2021 09:01 102 \n\nHi @david, I started to receive structured workouts (both running and riding) in intervals.icu, using heartrate as intensity target. Unfortunately the Garmin Connect workout upload from intervals seems incomplete:\n\n- the workout “steps-skeleton” is correct:\n- the steps, the repetitions and the durations seems OK,\n- but the intensity targets are completely missing.\n\nIs this a bug, or a feature?\n\n Gabor_Ziegler 8 December 2021 09:03 103 \n\nI mean, the intensity targets are missing not just in Connect calendar view, but also in my watch, too.\n\n david 8 December 2021 16:10 104 \n\nDid you use “Instant HR”?\n\n`- 30m 80% HR hr=1s`\n\n Screen Shot 2021-12-08 at 18.09.45 906×990 56.9 KB \n\n HR target missing from Garmin Connect workout upload \n\n Gabor_Ziegler 8 December 2021 16:53 105 \n\nYou mean if my coach used that? No idea. I can’t see such field as an athlete\n\n...\n\nIf you open the workout, then click/press on Add Steps, you will get to the screen David showed. All the guys I “coach” can edit their workouts and/or shift them if needed. \n\n Gabor_Ziegler 9 December 2021 22:52 108 \n\nOh (facepalm) I got it. It is text based input.\n\nI didn’t know that… I am new to intervals.\n\nSo no, not instant HR\n\n...\n\ndavid 31 December 2021 11:58 110 \n\nIntervals.icu only uploads the next week or so to GC. Otherwise it gets very cluttered. The goal is just to get them onto your Garmin device, not fill in the whole calendar.\n\n...\n\n@Michael_Jasper there are many small differences I belive but as @Antoinette_Tinker pointed out it probably does not matter It depends on what you want. For example if you are using our Coach Jack feature then we modify the workouts to make them better for outdoors when sending to Garmin. David pointed out about they send the next week there. We have the option to send more than one week but if sending from TrainerDay I recommend sending one week at a time because we create targets in exact watts based on your current FTP, this improves the Garmin experience. So it’s better to send less at a time in case your FTP changes. I belive Intervals is sendig % targets but I am not sure of that, Intervals might have the flexiibility to choose how you send them. So lots of small differences that may or may not matter to you. If you use CoachJack and are riding outdoors then I would send from TrainerDay.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='site:forum.intervals.icu October 2026 workout export format heart rate ranges' numResults=8\"","aggregated_output":"Title: Suunto guides: heart-rate step targets show no target and no gauge on the watch, pace targets do\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/suunto-guides-heart-rate-step-targets-show-no-target-and-no-gauge-on-the-watch-pace-targets-do/131169\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## What happens\n\nOn a workout synced to a Suunto 9 Peak, a step whose target is pace shows the step\nname, `Avg`, `Target`, the value and the gauge. A step whose target is heart rate\nshows the step name and the ordinary running fields only – no target, no range, no\ngauge, nothing that says what to hold.\n\nI could not tell from the watch whether this was my syntax, so I built one workout with\neleven steps, one target form each, and walked through all of them:\n\n| step | written as | on the watch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 zone | `90s Z3 HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 2 pctmax one | `90s 85% HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 3 pctmax rng | `90s 82-87% HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 4 thr one | `90s 90% LTHR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 5 thr rng | `90s 88-94% LTHR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 6 zone span | `90s Z2-Z3 HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 7 zone p | `90s Z2 Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 8 pct p one | `90s 40% Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 9 pct p rng | `90s 60-70% Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 10 abs p | `90s 7:00 pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 11 dist | `400mtr Z2 Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n\nAll six heart-rate forms behave identically. All five pace forms work.\n\n## Why I think the data is not the problem\n\nIntervals resolves every one of those HR steps to absolute bpm – they are shown that way\nin the workout view, and they are in the workout export\n(`/api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{id}/download.json`) as a `_hr` object:\n\n```auto\n1 zone \"_hr\": {\"start\": 153.0, \"end\": 163.0}\n2 pctmax one \"_hr\": {\"value\": 159.0, \"start\": 156.0, \"end\": 162.0}\n3 pctmax rng \"_hr\": {\"value\": 158.5, \"start\": 154.0, \"end\": 163.0}\n4 thr one \"_hr\": {\"value\": 154.0, \"start\": 151.0, \"end\": 157.0}\n5 thr rng \"_hr\": {\"value\": 156.0, \"start\": 151.0, \"end\": 161.0}\n6 zone span \"_hr\": {\"start\": 146.0, \"end\": 163.0}\n\n```\n\nAnd the SuuntoPlus Guide format has a field for exactly this shape. From Suunto’s own\nGuide Cloud API document, the sample guide’s warm-up step:\n\n```json\n\"fields\": [\n  { \"type\": \"targetHeartRate\", \"value\": 111, \"min\": 104, \"max\": 148 },\n  { \"type\": \"heartRate\" },\n  ...\n]\n\n```\n\n`targetHeartRate` takes absolute bpm as a value with optional min/max, and Suunto’s own\nexample uses it on a heart-rate step.\n\n## And the watch can display it\n\nA structured workout built in the Suunto app on the same watch, with heart-rate bands\n(warm-up 130-140, interval 150-160, recovery 100-110), shows the target and the gauge\nnormally during the heart-rate steps. So the watch and its target display are not the\nlimitation.\n\n...\n\nYou could try to set it in Intervals as ‘Auto’.\nI don’t know if a mix will then work in Suunto, but there was recently some work done to get this supported on Garmin.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Downloading planned workouts from the API\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/downloading-planned-workouts-from-the-api/93737\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nYou can download planned workouts from an athlete’s calendar in zwo (Zwift), fit, mrc and erg format (CALENDAR:READ scope required):\n\n```auto\n$ curl 'https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/0/events?category=WORKOUT&ext=zwo&resolve=true' \\\n    -H 'Authorization: Bearer d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9'\n\n```\n\nThe optional category parameter specifies what sort of events to return (planned workouts in this case). The ext parameter the format (leave out if you don’t need the file). The resolve=true parameter converts power (and heart rate etc.) from units like % of FTP into watts. The default date range is a week from today (use oldest and newest parameters in ISO-8601 format to change).\n\nExample abridged response:\n\n```auto\n[\n  {\n    \"id\": 46696127,\n    \"start_date_local\": \"2024-11-21T07:00:00\",\n    \"type\": \"Ride\",\n    \"category\": \"WORKOUT\",\n    \"name\": \"Big Gear Grinds\",\n    \"description\": \"...\",\n    ...\n    \"workout_doc\": {\n      \"description\": \"A great session for building cycling specific strength both in and out of the saddle.\",\n      \"target\": \"POWER\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        {\n          \"text\": \"Welcome to the ...\",\n          \"duration\": 180,\n          \"warmup\": true,\n          \"ramp\": true,\n          \"power\": { \"start\": 35.0, \"end\": 55.0, \"units\": \"%ftp\" },\n          \"cadence\": { \"value\": 85.0, \"units\": \"rpm\" },\n          \"_power\": { \"value\": 126.0, \"start\": 98.0, \"end\": 154.0 }\n        },\n      ],\n      ...\n    },\n    ...\n    \"workout_filename\": \"Big_Gear_Grinds.zwo\",\n    \"workout_file_base64\": \"PD94bWwgdmVy...\"\n  }\n]\n\n```\n\nThe workout_file_base64 field contains the file in the specified format (zwo in this case) encoded as base 64 text.\n\nThe workout_doc field contains the workout in native Intervals.icu format. If you don’t already have a parser for one of the other formats then this might be easier to work with.\n\nThis is how the workout_doc field is mapped in Intervals.icu:\n\n```java\npublic class WorkoutDoc {\n\n    public String description;\n    public Map<String, String> description_locale;\n    public Integer duration;\n    public Float distance;\n    public Integer ftp;\n    public Integer lthr;\n    public Float threshold_pace; // meters/sec\n    public String pace_units; // SECS_100M, SECS_100Y, MINS_KM, MINS_MILE, SECS_500M\n    public SportSettings sportSettings;\n    public String category;\n    public WorkoutTarget target;\n    public List<Step> steps;\n    public List<Object> zoneTimes; // sometimes array of ints otherwise array of objects\n    public Map<String, String> options;\n    public List<String> locales;\n\n    public enum Option {\n        category, pool_length, power\n    }\n\n    public static class Step {\n        public String text;\n        public Map<String, String> text_locale;\n        public Integer duration;\n        public Float distance;\n        public Boolean until_lap_press;\n        public Integer reps;\n        public Boolean warmup;\n        public Boolean cooldown;\n        public String intensity; // \"active\", \"rest\", \"warmup\", \"cooldown\", \"recovery\", \"interval\", \"other\"\n        public List<Step> steps;\n        public Boolean ramp;\n        public Boolean freeride;\n        public Boolean maxeffort;\n        public Value power;\n        public Value hr;\n        public Value pace;\n        public Value cadence;\n        public Boolean hidepower;\n\n        // these are filled in with actual watts, bpm etc. when resolve=true parameter is supplied to the endpoint\n        public Value _power;\n        public Value _hr;\n        public Value _pace;\n        public Float _distance;\n    }\n\n    public static class Value {\n        public Double value;\n        public Double start;\n        public Double end;\n        public String units;\n        public String target;\n        public Integer mmp_duration;\n    }\n}\n\n/** How should workouts be performed? Target power, HR or pace? */\npublic enum WorkoutTarget {\n    AUTO(\"A\"), POWER(\"W\"), HR(\"H\"), PACE(\"P\");\n}\n\n```\n\n---\n\nTitle: HR target workouts broken on Edge 840 firmware ≥30.18 — interpreted as %HRmax instead of bpm - Bug Reports - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/hr-target-workouts-broken-on-edge-840-firmware-30-18-interpreted-as-hrmax-instead-of-bpm/130090\nPublished: 2026-05-19T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## Summary\n\nAfter installing Edge 840 firmware 30.18 (changelog entry: “Aligned presentation of time in zones for power and heart rate with Garmin Connect”),\nstructured workouts pushed via the intervals.icu API with HR-bpm targets are misinterpreted by the device.\nThe Edge now displays the workout’s lower HR bound as `bpm_value × HRmax / 100`.\nFor an athlete with HR max 199 and a target of 122 bpm, the Edge alarms `\"HR below minimum 242 bpm\"` (= 122 % × 199 ≈ 242.8) continuously throughout the ride.\n\nThe same workout structure worked correctly on the Edge before the firmware update.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nPush a Z2 workout with HR-bpm target via `/events/bulk`:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST 'https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/0/events/bulk?upsert=true&upsertOnUid=false&updatePlanApplied=false' \\\n  -u \"API_KEY:$KEY\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '[{\n    \"external_id\": \"hr-bug-repro\",\n    \"category\": \"WORKOUT\",\n    \"type\": \"Ride\",\n    \"start_date_local\": \"2026-05-25T09:00:00\",\n    \"name\": \"HR bug repro\",\n    \"moving_time\": 3600,\n    \"description\": \"- 60m 122-147bpm Z2 endurance\",\n    \"workout_doc\": {\n      \"target\": \"HR\",\n      \"steps\": [{\n        \"intensity\": \"active\",\n        \"duration\": 3600,\n        \"hr\": {\"start\": 122, \"end\": 147, \"units\": \"bpm\"},\n        \"text\": \"Z2 endurance\"\n      }]\n    }\n  }]'\n\n```\n\nLoad the synced workout on an Edge 840 running firmware ≥30.18 and start the activity. The device alarms `\"HR below minimum 242 bpm\"` (where 242 = `122 × HRmax / 100`).\n\n## Investigation findings\n\nWhile debugging this on my own account, I observed that all public export endpoints strip HR-bpm targets entirely:\n\n| Export | HR-bpm result | Power-%FTP result (control) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `/events/{id}/download.zwo` | `` — no target attrs | `` |\n| `/events/{id}/download.mrc` | Empty `[COURSE DATA]` | Populated with target % values |\n| `/events/{id}/download.fit` | 195 bytes, no target zone | 204 bytes, with power target |\n\nThe `hr_zone` variant (`hr: {units: \"hr_zone\", value: 2}`) also strips on export — even though intervals.icu maintains a distinct HR-zone internal model (`zoneTimes` returns Z1–Z5 instead of the power-zone Z1–Z7 when `target: HR`).\n\nAdditionally, intervals.icu’s own Zwift importer drops HR targets : uploading a `.zwo` containing `` via `/folders/{id}/import-workout` results in a stored `workout_doc` where the step is replaced by `power: {units: \"%ftp\", value: 0}` — the HR target metadata is discarded entirely.\n\nThese observations suggest the `workout_doc` HR-target representation either has no Garmin-format translation in your codebase, or the internal Garmin Connect sync API path emits HR values without an explicit absolute-bpm unit marker — relying on a pre-30.18 lenient Edge default that no longer exists.\n\n## Suspected cause\n\nintervals.icu’s Garmin Connect sync emits HR target values without explicitly tagging them as absolute bpm. Pre-30.18, the Edge defaulted to “treat as bpm.” Post-30.18, the Edge follows Garmin Connect’s documented convention and defaults to “treat as %HRmax.” The numeric value passes through unchanged, but its meaning flips.\n\n## Workaround (for affected users)\n\nSwitch HR-target workouts to power-target with `{units: \"%ftp\"}` ranges — these survive both the export pipeline and the Garmin Connect sync correctly. For HR-ceiling enforcement on the Edge, use the device-level HR alert (Activity Profile → Alerts → Heart Rate → set ceiling), which works regardless of workout target.\n\n...\n\nThis is no valid intervals syntax. If you use the API the description should contain valid syntax, this is what will be parsed.\n\nAn example of correct syntax would be:\n\n```auto\n\"description\": \"- 122-147bpm 60m Z2 HR\",\n\n```\n\nBut intervals is not supporting absolute values. The bpm range is considered as text.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Workout builder - Page 24 - Guide - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/workout-builder/1163?page=24\nPublished: 2020-06-16T11:49:37.000Z\nAuthor: Howie\nHighlights:\nSure I will send you some files. For both Garmin and Suunto you supply a JSON doc.\n\n...\n\ndavid 16 April 2022 08:19 472 \n\nNo the format is quite different. But all the worked out power/bpm ranges in the JSON download will help.\n\n...\n\nIn my post here Building workout using zone number instead of percentage range - #24 by app4g,\n\nScreenshot 2022-04-17 at 10.35.43 PM 1216×1270 188 KB\n\nIt’s from this Test workout \n\nScreenshot 2022-04-17 at 10.28.44 PM 1432×778 50 KB\n\n```auto\n[\n  {\n    \"duration\": 300,\n    \"ramp\": true,\n    \"hr\": {\n      \"start\": 100,\n      \"end\": 50,\n      \"units\": \"%hr\"\n    },\n    \"_hr\": {\n      \"value\": 174,\n      \"start\": 116,\n      \"end\": 232\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"duration\": 300,\n    \"ramp\": true,\n    \"hr\": {\n      \"start\": 50,\n      \"end\": 100,\n      \"units\": \"%hr\"\n    },\n    \"_hr\": {\n      \"value\": 174,\n      \"start\": 116,\n      \"end\": 232\n    }\n  }\n]\n\n```\n\nGood news now is that the workout shown on Intervals will be similar as workout shown in BreakAway. HR Ramps will be plotted as a Ramp, but internally it’s a range. HR Range will be plotted as a flat line (ave of range) but internally will still be a range)\n\nHR Target Based Training feature, using workouts sync’ed from intervals.icu is finally nearly there.\n\n...\n\nFixed. Ramps are now kept for HR and pace. Will deploy Tues AM (GMT+2).\n\n...\n\nThe zone ranges go from the start of the zone to the end of the zone. So if you to “Z2-Z3” its lowest bpm (or watts) in Z2 to highest in Z3. Just using “Z2” is lowest in Z2 to highest in Z2.\n\nZ1 is a special case with no lower bound so 80% of the upper bound is used.\n\n---\n\nTitle: How to send structured workouts with repeats and pace/power ranges via API? - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/how-to-send-structured-workouts-with-repeats-and-pace-power-ranges-via-api/130971\nPublished: 2026-08-11T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nI tried this:\n\ncurl -X POST \\\n\n“ https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/i611360/events/bulk?upsert=true” \\\n\n-u “API_KEY:$TOKEN” \\\n\n-H “Content-Type: application/json” \\\n\n-d '[\n\n{\n\n“category”: “WORKOUT”,\n\n“start_date_local”: “2026-08-17T00:00:00”,\n\n“type”: “Run”,\n\n“name”: “TEST 2x1km Pace Range V2”,\n\n“description”: “- 10m 5:30-6:16/km Pace\\n\\n2x\\n- 1km 3:30-3:50/km Pace\\n- 2m 5:10-6:00/km Pace\\n\\n- 10m 5:30-6:16/km Pace”,\n\n“external_id”: “limiar-2x1km-pace-002”\n\n}\n\n]’\n\n...\n\n> @Workout Builder Syntax Quick Guide:\n> \n> Intervals.icu Workout Builder Syntax Quick Guide Use this as a short cheat sheet when writing workout lines. 1) Basic line format Most steps follow this pattern: - [duration OR distance] [target] [optional cadence] Examples: - 5m30s 60% 90rpm - 1km 70% HR - 500mtr 5:00/km Pace 2) Duration and distance Time Hours: 1h Minutes: 10m, 5m Seconds: 30s, 90s Combined: 1h2m30s, 5m30s Short form: 5', 30\", 1'30\" Distance Metric: 500mtr, 2km, 10km Imperial: 1mi, 4.5mi Important m means minutes (…\n\n...\n\nThat’s intentional. Both Pace and Power targets are sent as a % of threshold. This makes workouts portable over longer time (where threshold changes) and in between athletes with different thresholds.\nSo you need threshold and zones to be configured.\n\n...\n\nActually, I observed something different. If I send a specific pace range, such as 3:55/km–4:10/km, and don’t have pace zones configured in Intervals.icu, the pace range is ignored. But if I configure any pace value (for example, 6:00/km), the specific 3:55–4:10/km range is sent correctly. So it seems that the configured zone is required, but the workout target itself remains an absolute pace range rather than being converted to a percentage.\n\n...\n\nYou can use absolute pace and power values but for the reasons above, it is discouraged.\nYou need to set threshold and zones in Intervals to get correct load and full analysis. No way around that.\n\nI don’t know exactly how absolute Pace is handled but for absolute Power (on Garmin - not every brand), you have to send a value over 1000. Any value below 1000 is interpreted as a percentage of threshold. For values above 1000, the first digit (the 1) is dropped and the rest is used as absolute power target value.\n\n- 50 = 50%\n- 100 = 100%\n- 400 = 400%\n- 1050 = 50W\n- 1100 = 100W\n- 11100 = 1100W\n\nBut without threshold and zones, Intervals can’t attribute a Load to a workout with absolute values, so it won’t work.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Workout Builder Syntax Quick Guide - Guide - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/workout-builder-syntax-quick-guide/123701\nPublished: 2026-03-03T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## 1) Basic line format\n\nMost steps follow this pattern:\n\n```plaintext\n- [duration OR distance] [target] [optional cadence]\n\n```\n\nExamples:\n\n```plaintext\n- 5m30s 60% 90rpm\n- 1km 70% HR\n- 500mtr 5:00/km Pace\n\n```\n\n...\n\n### Time\n\n- Hours: `1h`\n- Minutes: `10m`, `5m`\n- Seconds: `30s`, `90s`\n- Combined: `1h2m30s`, `5m30s`\n- Short form: `5'`, `30\"`, `1'30\"`\n\n### Distance\n\n- Metric: `500mtr`, `2km`, `10km`\n- Imperial: `1mi`, `4.5mi`\n\n> Important\n> \n> - `m` means minutes (not meters).\n> - For meters, use `mtr`.\n\n## 3) Targets\n\n### Power\n\n- FTP percentage: `75%`, `95-105%`\n- Absolute power: `220w`, `200-240w`\n- Zones: `Z2`, `Z3-Z4`\n- MMP: `60% MMP 5m`, `50-60% MMP 3m`\n- Custom zones: `CZ1`, `CZ2-CZ3`\n\n### Heart rate\n\n- Percent of max HR: `70% HR`, `75-80% HR`\n- Percent of threshold HR: `95% LTHR`, `90-95% LTHR`\n- HR zones: `Z2 HR`, `Z2-Z3 HR`\n\n### Pace\n\n- Percent of threshold pace: `60% Pace`, `78-82% Pace`\n- Pace zones: `Z2 Pace`, `Z2-Z3 Pace`\n- Absolute pace: `5:00 Pace`, `5:00/km Pace`, `3:00/100m-4:00/100m Pace`\n\n> Pace Note\n> \n> - Absolute pace is written as `mm:ss` per distance unit.\n> - Common units: `/100m`, `/100y`, `/km`, `/mi`, `/500m`, `/400m`, `/250m`.\n> - If you omit the distance unit, the sport default from settings is used.\n\n...\n\nAdd cadence after the target:\n\n```plaintext\n- 10m 75% 90rpm\n- 12m 85% 90-100rpm\n\n---\n\nTitle: Incorrect structured workout rendering in calendar export - Bug Reports - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/incorrect-structured-workout-rendering-in-calendar-export/127096\nPublished: 2026-04-13T16:16:03.000Z\nAuthor: MedTechCD\nHighlights:\nHere is what I created in the workout editor:\n\n```auto\nWarmup:\n- 2mi @ Z1 HR\n\n2x Intervals:\n- Work 6m @ 92-96% LTHR\n- Recover 1m\n\nCooldown:\n- 2mi @ Z1 HR\n\n```\n\nWhen I close the editor, I see this:\n\n```auto\nWarmup:\n\n2mi @ Z1 HR (83-104bpm)\n2x Intervals:\n\nWork 6m @ 92-96% LTHR (120-125bpm)\nRecover 1m\nCooldown:\n\n2mi @ Z1 HR (83-104bpm)\n\n```\n\nNote the added bpm ranges in the rendered text, which is a nice touch. In fact (feature request?), it would be nice to be able to specify bpm ranges in the editor, but I couldn’t get that to work.\n\nBut now, in my Google Calendar entry (via the exported workout calendar), I see this is the description text:\n\n```auto\nWarmup:\n- 2mi @ Z1 HR\n\n2x Intervals:\n- Work 6m @ 221-230w LTHR\n- Recover 1m\n\nCooldown:\n- 2mi @ Z1 HR\n\n39 training load\n\n```\n\nThe line `Work 6m @ 221-230w LTHR` is non-sensical, no? What is `221-230w LTHR`?\n\n(My suggestion would be to have the calendar entry note include the raw workout note text verbatim…)\n\n...\n\nNo worries. Thanks for pointing me to the guide.\n\n...\n\nI did not find any mention of bpm syntax specifications, though. Searching for “bpm” turned up nothing. I tried specifications like “120bpm” and “110-120bpm”, but they didn’t work. The editor tells you, in a sense, if your syntax is correct, because the graph at the bottom will immediately go from blank to filled in.\n\n MedTechCD 15 April 2026 11:23 4 \n\nYou are absolutely right. HR is only available as a % of HRmax, % of LTHR or as Zone. Power can be specified by absolute numbers but HR not.\n\n> The line `Work 6m @ 221-230w LTHR` is non-sensical, no? What is `221-230w LTHR`?\n\nI suspect that the calendar export is not correctly interpreting % LTHR and just takes the power percentages as target. If 221W - 230W correspond to 92% FTP and 96% FTP, that would confirm my suspicion. (Your FTP then being 240W).\n\n---\n\nTitle: Import and download workout and activity FIT files\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/import-and-download-workout-and-activity-fit-files/19295\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nDownload of activities in FIT format is now supported. The downloaded file includes whatever changes you may have made to the power and heartrate data. So you can fix data in Intervals.icu and upload to other services. Choose to include power and/or heartrate data and related stats in the downloaded file or not. This option is available on the activity data page.\n\nYou can now import workout FIT files directly into the workout editor or into a folder in your workout library. The currently selected athlete’s settings are used for the import.\n\nWorkouts can now be downloaded in FIT format from the workout library and directly from the calendar. The currently selected athlete’s FTP, LTHR and other settings are used to create the workout file. Note that automatic upload via Garmin Connect is still supported.\n\n...\n\nIt´s on the Activity Intervals page.\nOpen the activity and the menu will have Power, (Pace), HR and Intervals pages for that specific activity.\n\n...\n\nNo, all that information is still valid. You just need to open the correct page… If you want the FIT file of one specific activity, open the activity, then select the Activity Interval Data page and you will find them:\n\nIf you want the fit files from multiple activities at once, @nasatt 's instructions are a better option.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='site:forum.intervals.icu October 2026 Garmin HR target instant range workout export start end' numResults=8\"","aggregated_output":"Title: Suunto guides: heart-rate step targets show no target and no gauge on the watch, pace targets do\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/suunto-guides-heart-rate-step-targets-show-no-target-and-no-gauge-on-the-watch-pace-targets-do/131169\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## What happens\n\nOn a workout synced to a Suunto 9 Peak, a step whose target is pace shows the step\nname, `Avg`, `Target`, the value and the gauge. A step whose target is heart rate\nshows the step name and the ordinary running fields only – no target, no range, no\ngauge, nothing that says what to hold.\n\nI could not tell from the watch whether this was my syntax, so I built one workout with\neleven steps, one target form each, and walked through all of them:\n\n| step | written as | on the watch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 zone | `90s Z3 HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 2 pctmax one | `90s 85% HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 3 pctmax rng | `90s 82-87% HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 4 thr one | `90s 90% LTHR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 5 thr rng | `90s 88-94% LTHR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 6 zone span | `90s Z2-Z3 HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 7 zone p | `90s Z2 Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 8 pct p one | `90s 40% Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 9 pct p rng | `90s 60-70% Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 10 abs p | `90s 7:00 pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 11 dist | `400mtr Z2 Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n\nAll six heart-rate forms behave identically. All five pace forms work.\n\n## Why I think the data is not the problem\n\nIntervals resolves every one of those HR steps to absolute bpm – they are shown that way\nin the workout view, and they are in the workout export\n(`/api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{id}/download.json`) as a `_hr` object:\n\n```auto\n1 zone \"_hr\": {\"start\": 153.0, \"end\": 163.0}\n2 pctmax one \"_hr\": {\"value\": 159.0, \"start\": 156.0, \"end\": 162.0}\n3 pctmax rng \"_hr\": {\"value\": 158.5, \"start\": 154.0, \"end\": 163.0}\n4 thr one \"_hr\": {\"value\": 154.0, \"start\": 151.0, \"end\": 157.0}\n5 thr rng \"_hr\": {\"value\": 156.0, \"start\": 151.0, \"end\": 161.0}\n6 zone span \"_hr\": {\"start\": 146.0, \"end\": 163.0}\n\n```\n\nAnd the SuuntoPlus Guide format has a field for exactly this shape. From Suunto’s own\nGuide Cloud API document, the sample guide’s warm-up step:\n\n```json\n\"fields\": [\n  { \"type\": \"targetHeartRate\", \"value\": 111, \"min\": 104, \"max\": 148 },\n  { \"type\": \"heartRate\" },\n  ...\n]\n\n```\n\n`targetHeartRate` takes absolute bpm as a value with optional min/max, and Suunto’s own\nexample uses it on a heart-rate step.\n\n## And the watch can display it\n\nA structured workout built in the Suunto app on the same watch, with heart-rate bands\n(warm-up 130-140, interval 150-160, recovery 100-110), shows the target and the gauge\nnormally during the heart-rate steps. So the watch and its target display are not the\nlimitation.\n\n...\n\nthank you! I built a workout with ONLY heart-rate steps and all three show\nthe target and the dial, perfectly.\n\n...\n\nYou could try to set it in Intervals as ‘Auto’.\nI don’t know if a mix will then work in Suunto, but there was recently some work done to get this supported on Garmin.\n\nIt’s strange that we don’t see those selectors on your screenshot. If you select HR, the Intervals graph should show the workout coloured in HR zones. If Pace is selected, it would be in Pace zone colors. Auto will show it depending on your priority settings.\n\n---\n\nTitle: HR target missing from Garmin Connect workout upload\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/hr-target-missing-from-garmin-connect-workout-upload/5483\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nThe Garmin Connect workout upload from intervals are incomplete:\n\n- the workout “steps-skeleton” is correct:\n- the steps, the repetitions and the durations seems OK,\n- but the HR intensity targets are completely missing.\n\n...\n\nTx for all this info. From the other thread:\n\n> @Upload planned workouts to Garmin Connect:\n> \n> Did you use “Instant HR”? - 30m 80% HR hr=1s [Screen Shot 2021-12-08 at 18.09.45]\n\n...\n\nLook for the “hr=1s” on steps. It doesn’t look like your workouts have that.\n\n...\n\nIt’s not a bug and it’s not a workaround. The screen tells you that you have to choose Instant HR for Garmin watch.\nBut there are people using something else then Garmin watch… The other settings are for head units, Edge for example.\nI’m not sure, but possibly it is enough to make the choice ones to make it default on your account.\n\n...\n\nGarmin Connect knows what device you have.\nYou don’t have to “type” ‘hr=1s’. You can but you can also choose the Instant HR option in the workout builder.\nDid you search the forum for both terms “Instant HR” and “Lap HR”?\nThat might explain quite a bit.\n\n...\n\nHmm. Maybe I should change the default. I initially went with Lap HR because that makes sense for cycling and thats where Intervals.icu started. I think instant HR would also be ok for cycling. Your HR doesn’t change all that quickly.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Power and HR targets for Garmin workouts\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/power-and-hr-targets-for-garmin-workouts/2926\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nYou can now specify power and HR targets for workout steps (default is lap power and lap HR):\n\nThis sorts out the issue with HR steps not working on Garmin watches (well at least some of them). You need to choose “Instant HR” (hr=1s in the text):\n\n- warmup 30s 50% LTHR hr=1s\n- Wibble 20m 78% LTHR hr=1s\n- cooldown 30s 40% LTHR hr=1s\n\n...\n\nUnfortunately the target in the Garmin API is just “PACE”. There aren’t other options like there are for power.\n\n...\n\n@david Not sure if anyone else has been successful, but I finally managed to create a structured workout with my Stryd power targets and did it this morning. Below the syntax used, basically the instructions on Garmin during the execution (audio and on watch) were good enough for me to follow the structure. But some tweaks still needed:\n-When displaying the workout on Garmin watch (going in calendar, selecting the workout, then display/preview), for each split I have “Power 3s” prefix and then the lower target level set, instead of the specific power range defined, like I have when I configure a workout from Stryd and push it to Garmin\n-When uploading the FIT back to Intervals, it has associated correctly the activity to the planned workout… But the intervals, although well synchronized (I had some “Press Lap” splits) the type of intervals are not correct. How to properly force specific intervals to be detected as “Work” and others as “Recovery”? Actually same issue in Garmin Connect when looking at the activity, everything but the cooldown at the end is displayed as Work.\n\nBelow the syntax of the workout (sorry for the french text in-line) - note that I used Google Gemini to get some help to write the structure, but had to iterate a couple of times until it was displaying properly and also looking good on the watch when pushing to Garmin):\n\n```auto\nWarmup\n- Course facile 12m 70-80% FTP\n\nActivation 2x\n- Activation 1m 97-103% FTP\n- Récup 2m 70-80% FTP\n\nTransition\n- Transition 2m 70-80% FTP\n\nMain Set 5x\n- 15s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 15s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n- 20s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 20s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n- 30s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 30s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n- 20s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 20s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n- 15s 115-130% FTP\n- Descente 15s Press Lap 65-80% FTP\n\nCooldown\n- Retour au calme 10m 65-80% FTP\n\n```\n\n---\n\nTitle: Workout builder - Page 17 - Guide - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/workout-builder/1163?page=17\nPublished: 2021-02-15T15:35:40.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nUsing non “range” targets on Garmin’s workout page is impossible due the fact the gauge has a range. Without a range the pointer keeps jumping around like crazy mainly with power.\n\n...\n\nBecause Garmin is not the one controlling the trainer and the app that does that needs a file uploaded to it:\n\n1. we create non range workouts and you add an automatic % range up and down when synchronizing with Garmin that needs that and the downloaded workouts would remain unchanged for use ERG on controlling apps.\n2. Vice-Versa. we create range files targeting the use on Garmin’s and you would take the average when downloading the workouts for ERG use.\n3. at least to put a warning in the steps of the workout builder to always choose “RANGE” for power and hr when using a Garmin like you you do for HR : \"If you are using a Garmin watch you must use “Instant HR”. But on this way we would have to create one workout for Garmin and other for using on ERG mode.\n\n...\n\nThere is already support for automatically making workouts uploaded to Garmin use a range:\n\nScreen Shot 2021-02-15 at 17.57.36 460×526 51.3 KB\n\nJust click the numbers to change the % of the range. This is applied to all fixed power/HR targets.\n\n...\n\nThe power range is only applied to workouts that are not tagged “indoor”. The assumption is that if you are indoor you are going to be controlling a smart trainer or something. Its easy to just not mark the activity as indoor if you are going to be uploading it to Garmin and want the range feature.\n\n---\n\nTitle: HR target workouts broken on Edge 840 firmware ≥30.18 — interpreted as %HRmax instead of bpm - Bug Reports - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/hr-target-workouts-broken-on-edge-840-firmware-30-18-interpreted-as-hrmax-instead-of-bpm/130090\nPublished: 2026-05-19T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n# HR target workouts broken on Edge 840 firmware ≥30.18 — interpreted as %HRmax instead of bpm\n\n## Summary\n\nAfter installing Edge 840 firmware 30.18 (changelog entry: “Aligned presentation of time in zones for power and heart rate with Garmin Connect”),\nstructured workouts pushed via the intervals.icu API with HR-bpm targets are misinterpreted by the device.\nThe Edge now displays the workout’s lower HR bound as `bpm_value × HRmax / 100`.\nFor an athlete with HR max 199 and a target of 122 bpm, the Edge alarms `\"HR below minimum 242 bpm\"` (= 122 % × 199 ≈ 242.8) continuously throughout the ride.\n\nThe same workout structure worked correctly on the Edge before the firmware update.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nPush a Z2 workout with HR-bpm target via `/events/bulk`:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST 'https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/0/events/bulk?upsert=true&upsertOnUid=false&updatePlanApplied=false' \\\n  -u \"API_KEY:$KEY\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '[{\n    \"external_id\": \"hr-bug-repro\",\n    \"category\": \"WORKOUT\",\n    \"type\": \"Ride\",\n    \"start_date_local\": \"2026-05-25T09:00:00\",\n    \"name\": \"HR bug repro\",\n    \"moving_time\": 3600,\n    \"description\": \"- 60m 122-147bpm Z2 endurance\",\n    \"workout_doc\": {\n      \"target\": \"HR\",\n      \"steps\": [{\n        \"intensity\": \"active\",\n        \"duration\": 3600,\n        \"hr\": {\"start\": 122, \"end\": 147, \"units\": \"bpm\"},\n        \"text\": \"Z2 endurance\"\n      }]\n    }\n  }]'\n\n```\n\nLoad the synced workout on an Edge 840 running firmware ≥30.18 and start the activity. The device alarms `\"HR below minimum 242 bpm\"` (where 242 = `122 × HRmax / 100`).\n\n## Investigation findings\n\nWhile debugging this on my own account, I observed that all public export endpoints strip HR-bpm targets entirely:\n\n| Export | HR-bpm result | Power-%FTP result (control) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `/events/{id}/download.zwo` | `` — no target attrs | `` |\n| `/events/{id}/download.mrc` | Empty `[COURSE DATA]` | Populated with target % values |\n| `/events/{id}/download.fit` | 195 bytes, no target zone | 204 bytes, with power target |\n\nThe `hr_zone` variant (`hr: {units: \"hr_zone\", value: 2}`) also strips on export — even though intervals.icu maintains a distinct HR-zone internal model (`zoneTimes` returns Z1–Z5 instead of the power-zone Z1–Z7 when `target: HR`).\n\nAdditionally, intervals.icu’s own Zwift importer drops HR targets : uploading a `.zwo` containing `` via `/folders/{id}/import-workout` results in a stored `workout_doc` where the step is replaced by `power: {units: \"%ftp\", value: 0}` — the HR target metadata is discarded entirely.\n\nThese observations suggest the `workout_doc` HR-target representation either has no Garmin-format translation in your codebase, or the internal Garmin Connect sync API path emits HR values without an explicit absolute-bpm unit marker — relying on a pre-30.18 lenient Edge default that no longer exists.\n\n## Suspected cause\n\nintervals.icu’s Garmin Connect sync emits HR target values without explicitly tagging them as absolute bpm. Pre-30.18, the Edge defaulted to “treat as bpm.” Post-30.18, the Edge follows Garmin Connect’s documented convention and defaults to “treat as %HRmax.” The numeric value passes through unchanged, but its meaning flips.\n\n## Workaround (for affected users)\n\nSwitch HR-target workouts to power-target with `{units: \"%ftp\"}` ranges — these survive both the export pipeline and the Garmin Connect sync correctly. For HR-ceiling enforcement on the Edge, use the device-level HR alert (Activity Profile → Alerts → Heart Rate → set ceiling), which works regardless of workout target.\n\n...\n\nThis is no valid intervals syntax. If you use the API the description should contain valid syntax, this is what will be parsed.\n\nAn example of correct syntax would be:\n\n```auto\n\"description\": \"- 122-147bpm 60m Z2 HR\",\n\n```\n\nBut intervals is not supporting absolute values. The bpm range is considered as text.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Syncing Pace & HR targets to Garmin - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/syncing-pace-hr-targets-to-garmin/130238\nPublished: 2026-06-01T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nNew to intervals.icu and trying to understand the workout syntax a bit better. I’m using my own dev tool to push my plan to intervals.icu with the API, that then syncs it to my Garmin watch. But having a bit of trouble getting the pace targets to sync. Garmin shows ‘No targets’ for my repeats. Currently, this is how my example run looks like\n\n```auto\n- 22m Z2 HR\n\n4x\n- Strides 20s Z4-Z5 Pace\n- Walk 60s\n\n```\n\nInterestingly, removing the first 22mins and just having the whole workout as 4x repeats somehow seems to be working. Any help with this would be appreciated 🙂\n\n...\n\nEdit: Is it with combining multiple targets?\n\nI changed the ‘Auto’ to ‘Pace’ and the pace synced well for the strides, but HR for easy run says ‘No Target’ now.\n\n...\n\nIntervals hasn’t figured out yet how to send workouts over the Garmin API with multiple targets.\nLet’s be clear: Intervals is NOT sending FIT workouts to Garmin because Garmin doesn’t allow that. Intervals sends a workout in a script format to the Garmin API and Garmin Connect translates that to a workout on your device. The target type definition is not clear or Garmin is somehow blocking that.\nThis is kind of a hot topic over the last couple of months but the solution hasn’t been found yet.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Custom Heart Rate range planned workout - Training - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/custom-heart-rate-range-planned-workout/72460\nPublished: 2024-07-23T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nYou need to specify the HR range in % of threshold HR. This is so workouts are portable between athletes. So if threshold HR is 170 bpm then use 130/170 = 76%. So “- 10m 74-76% HR”.\n\n...\n\nAlso possible with % of maxHR (which could give a slightly wider range of bpm).\n\n...\n\nTry not get fixated on an abolute value, as your physiology doesn’t work on on/off switches. It can change day to day, depending on your sleep, hydration, temprature (cooling) and more. Having said that, for the aerobic threshold test/workout that he refers to, limit +/-2bpm, you can use %maxHR, like follows:\n\n-30m 70-71% HR\n\nThis will be 2-3 bpm, maybe 2-4 if the maxHR is low.\nI’m using example values only.\n\n...\n\nBut note that power and RPE will fluctuate irrespective of how you feel, due to the wind, elevation, temperature, hydration, sleep all affecting HR.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Garmin Workout Screen - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/garmin-workout-screen/3422\nPublished: 2021-03-23T19:54:25.000Z\nAuthor: Basti_Sternburg\nHighlights:\nThis post explains what you need I think.\n\n> You can now specify power and HR targets for workout steps (default is lap power and lap HR): [Screen Shot 2021-02-14 at 06.57.18] [Screen Shot 2021-02-14 at 06.58.29] This sorts out the issue with HR steps not working on Garmin watches (well at least some of them). You need to choose “Instant HR” (hr=1s in the text): warmup 30s 50% LTHR hr=1s Wibble 20m 78% LTHR hr=1s cooldown 30s 40% LTHR hr=1s [IMG_4164]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
